        "topicId": "math-y9-changing-subject",
        "stem": "A formula is $y=(kx+6)/4$ and another is $z=(2x-1)/3$. At x=2, y=4. Define $w=y-2z$. Which option makes x the subject in terms of w and gives the input for which y is exactly 1/2 more than twice z?",
        "options": [
          {
            "latex": "x=12w-26;\\ x=-20",
            "errorPath": "Reverse the subtraction when isolating x.",
            "id": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "B",
            "latex": "x=10-12w;\\ x=20",
            "errorPath": "Use −8 rather than +8 while expanding −2z in the symbolic difference, but solve the original numerical equality correctly."
          },
          {
            "latex": "x=26+12w;\\ x=32",
            "errorPath": "Move 12w with wrong sign.",
            "id": "C"
          },
          {
            "latex": "x=26-12w;\\ x=14",
            "errorPath": "Treat the half-unit excess as one unit.",
            "id": "D"
          },
          {
            "latex": "x=(26-12w)/2;\\ x=10",
            "errorPath": "Divide by2 instead of coefficient1.",
            "id": "E"
          },
          {
            "latex": "x=26-12w;\\ x=20",
            "id": "F"
          }
        ],
        "correctOptionId": "F",
        "correctAnswerLatex": "x=26-12w;\\ x=20",
        "workedSolutionSteps": [
          "k5 from 2k+6=16. Then 12w=15x+18−16x+8=26−x.",
          "Thus x=26−12w.",
          "The condition y=2z+1/2 means w=1/2, giving x20."
        ],
