        "topicId": "math-y9-changing-subject",
        "stem": "Machine A takes an input x to $(3x+k)/2$, and B takes an input x to $(2x-5)/3$. For the same starting input, applying B then A gives 9, while applying A then B gives 7. Let w denote the result of B then A after k has been determined. Which option gives x in terms of w and the result of A then B when w=12?",
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "A",
            "latex": "x=w+6;\\ 10",
            "errorPath": "Move the offset 6 with the wrong sign in the inverse, but find the reverse output from the two original chains separately."
          },
          {
            "latex": "x=w-4;\\ 12",
            "errorPath": "Use reverse-chain inverse x=w−4; x8, reverse output12.",
            "id": "B"
          },
          {
            "latex": "x=w-6;\\ 12",
            "errorPath": "Assume the chains commute.",
            "id": "C"
          },
          {
            "latex": "x=w-6;\\ 6",
            "errorPath": "Report original input for w12.",
            "id": "D"
          },
          {
            "latex": "x=w-6;\\ 10",
            "id": "E"
          },
          {
            "latex": "x=w-6;\\ 4",
            "errorPath": "Report reverse-chain offset instead of result.",
            "id": "F"
          }
        ],
        "correctOptionId": "E",
        "correctAnswerLatex": "x=w-6;\\ 10",
        "workedSolutionSteps": [
          "B then A gives w=x+(k−5)/2; A then B gives x+(k−5)/3.",
          "Their difference2=(k−5)/6, hence k17. Thus x=w−6 and reverse output x+4.",
          "For w12, x6, reverse output10."
        ],
